+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Allocation Failure:** The allocation policy must strictly sum to $B$. If the policy outputs non-integer or negative values, they must be rounded/clamped and adjusted to satisfy the sum constraint [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Neighbor Search Degeneracy:** If $B$ is very small, the hierarchical neighbor search may fail to find $K_{gs}$ distinct neighbors. The implementation must handle cases where fewer than $K_{gs}$ neighbors exist [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Training Stability:** Removing the projection loss causes training to fail entirely, as it ties anchors to image regions [S1, unofficial]. Removing the Voronoi loss causes a significant PSNR drop (2.51 dB) because Gaussians leave their anchor's cell and the hierarchical search retrieves incorrect neighbors [S1, unofficial].
+
+## Subtopics
+
+### Gaussian Allocation Policy
+A learned policy that distributes the Gaussian budget $B$ across anchors based on local scene significance (e.g., texture complexity). It differs from uniform allocation by concentrating capacity in high-frequency regions. Choose this when you need adaptive resource distribution. [Link to [Gaussian Allocation Policy](16746-feed-forward-generalizable-reconstruction.md#gaussian-allocation-policy)] (Note: Specific sub-article not provided in tree, linked to parent context).
+
+### Resource-Linear Decoder
+A decoder that refines Gaussians via local cross-attention with hierarchical neighbor search, ensuring $O(B \cdot K_{gs})$ complexity. Choose this when you need inference cost to scale linearly with $B$. [Link to [Resource-Linear Decoder](16746-feed-forward-generalizable-reconstruction.md#resource-linear-decoder)] (Note: Specific sub-article not provided in tree, linked to parent context).
+
 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity:** The decoding cost is $O(B \cdot K_{gs})$, scaling linearly with the Gaussian budget $B$, compared to global self-attention which costs $O(B^2)$ [S1, unofficial]. The hierarchical neighbor search further reduces the constant factor [S1, unofficial].
--   **Memory Complexity:** Peak memory scales linearly with $B$ [S1, unofficial].
--   **Performance:** At a budget of 2K Gaussians, RLSplat achieves higher PSNR than F4 Splat while using 9.6× less peak memory and running 4.1× faster [S1, unofficial]. On the ACID dataset ($B=8,192$), RLSplat achieves PSNR 22.84 dB, SSIM 0.643, LPIPS 0.353 [S1, unofficial]. On RE10K ($B=8,192$), RLSplat achieves full-view PSNR 26.51 dB, compared to 23.42 dB for F4 Splat and 21.82 dB for EcoSplat [S1, unofficial].
--   **Strengths:** Linear scaling of inference cost with budget; better allocation of Gaussians to high-frequency structures; no need for retraining to change $B$ (except for $K_{gs}$ which can be changed at inference) [S1, unofficial].
--   **Limitations:** Requires training of the allocation policy and decoder; hierarchical neighbor search adds complexity to implementation [S1, unofficial].
--   **When to use:** Applications requiring limited resource budgets, such as AR/VR, robotics, and digital content creation, where adjustable rendering quality is needed [S1, unofficial].
